# Template for the prompt handed to extraction agents

Copy the text below, fill in the `{{ }}` placeholders for your project, then hand it to each agent.
**Give every agent the exact same "shared section."** If this isn't consistent across agents, paths that cross layer boundaries won't merge.

---

## Shared section (identical for every agent — distribute unmodified)

```
You are examining the code at {{absolute path to the repository}} and producing,
for every user action (or automatic trigger), which code runs in what order,
as a machine-readable graph JSON.

## Absolute rules
- Never guess. Write only what you've confirmed by actually Reading / Grepping the real files.
  Function names, endpoints, topic names, and table names must match the real thing character-for-character.
- Attach a ref ("path relative to the repo:line number") to every node. The line number must be
  the actual definition line you confirmed. This feeds both the later hallucination check and
  duplicate detection, so always fill it in.
- Write function names exactly as they appear in the real code. Don't drop a private function's
  leading underscore (don't turn _on_cmd into on_cmd).
- label / detail are in {{language}}. label is a short noun phrase, 22 characters or fewer
  (short enough to fit inside a diagram box). detail is 120 characters or fewer, describing
  concretely what the step does. Include conditions, branches, retries, and failure behavior.

## Node ID naming convention (lowercase, ASCII, dot-separated)
- ui.<screen>.<action>            A button the user clicks / a UI action
- app.<module>.<function>         Client-side internals (hooks, stores, API clients, screen components)
- net.http.<METHOD>_<path>        The client→server HTTP boundary. Replace / and - in the path with _
- api.<handler function name>     Server-side request handler
- svc.<module>.<function>         Server-side internal processing
- db.<real table name>            Database
- gcs.<region>                    Object storage
- mqtt.<topic abbreviation>       Messaging (put the real topic string in detail)
- dev.<module>.<function>         Device / edge side
- hw.<device>                     Physical device
- ext.<external>                  External service
- job.<name>                      Scheduled / long-running process
- rt.<subscription target>        Realtime subscription

## Shared hub IDs (if your assigned area passes through one of these, use this exact spelling,
character for character. Never invent an alias.)
{{List the nodes that form layer boundaries here, using their real names. 5-15 of them. Example:
svc.mqtt_client.publish_command / mqtt.cmd / mqtt.cmdres / mqtt.event
dev.main._on_cmd / dev.uploader.upload
net.http.POST_upload / api.upload
app.lib.api.request / rt.events
}}

## lane values
{{This project's lanes. Default is the following 11}}
"ui" / "app" / "api" / "svc" / "job" / "db" / "store" / "mqtt" / "device" / "hw" / "ext"

## flows (the unit that maps to a button)
- 1 flow = "one button the user clicks" or "one automatic trigger."
- category must always be chosen from the following: {{list 8-10 categories. Example:
  Auth & Onboarding / Orders & Purchases / Inventory & Shipping / Settings Changes /
  Notifications / History / Maintenance / Automated & Scheduled Processes}}
- steps is "an ordered sequence of edges." Each step is {from, to, label, branch}.
  branch is "main" (happy path) / "alt" (conditional branch, alternate route) /
  "error" (failure, retry, timeout).
- Always include the asynchronous return leg too (the result comes back, the DB gets updated,
  the screen reflects it, etc.) and close the flow all the way to the end. Never leave it as a one-way trip.
- In notes, write down the gotchas, timeout values, and what the user sees when it fails.

## Output
1. Write the JSON file with Write to {{output directory}}/<KEY>.json.
   {"nodes":[{"id","lane","label","detail","ref"}...],
    "edges":[{"from","to","label","kind"}...],
    "flows":[{"id","title","category","trigger","steps":[{"from","to","label","branch"}...],"notes":[...]}]}
   kind is "call"/"http"/"mqtt"/"db"/"storage"/"push"/"email"/"timer"/"realtime"/"retry"/"error".
   flows[].id uses lowercase letters and hyphens. title is in {{language}}.
   edges must include every edge used in steps (duplicates are fine).
   nodes must include every ID referenced anywhere in steps/edges, with none missing.
   No trailing commas, no comments, no BOM. It must always be parseable.
2. Keep the final text (return value) short: the file path you wrote, the node count, the
   edge count, the list of flow ids, and only warnings about anything you weren't confident in.
   Never return the JSON body itself.
   (Returning the body would overflow the orchestrator's context.)
```

## Per-agent section (swap this in per agent)

```
## Your assigned key <KEY> = "{{key}}"
## Assigned area: {{title}}
{{List the files you're responsible for concretely, and describe what you want turned into flows.
 Being explicit about "which buttons must not be missed" improves accuracy.}}

You may also read files outside your assigned area, to the extent needed to build boundary
nodes when a flow crosses layers. But leave the detailed internal expansion to whoever owns
that area, and connect through the shared hub ID instead.
Produce at least 6 flows, ideally 10 or more. Never miss a button the user can click.
```

## Schema for structured output (for the return value)

If you can constrain the agent's return value with a type, use the following.

```json
{
  "type": "object",
  "required": ["file", "nodeCount", "edgeCount", "flowIds"],
  "properties": {
    "file":       {"type": "string"},
    "nodeCount":  {"type": "number"},
    "edgeCount":  {"type": "number"},
    "flowIds":    {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}},
    "hubIdsUsed": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}},
    "warnings":   {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}}
  }
}
```

Never take `warnings` lightly. In one real case, the extraction agent itself wrote in its
warnings that "the button for this action doesn't exist on the screen side (zero grep hits),"
and that finding was later reconfirmed as a critical issue during verification.

---

## Prompt handed to the verification agent

Once extraction is done, have **a different agent** verify it. Having the same agent who did
the extraction verify its own work won't surface the flaws.

```
You are an adversarial reviewer verifying a set of flow-graph JSON files for {{repository}}.
The JSON files live in {{output directory}}/ under these keys: {{file list}}
Each file has the shape {"nodes":[...], "edges":[...], "flows":[...]}.

Read the JSON files you need, cross-check them against the real repository code, and surface
factual errors, broken links, and ID inconsistencies.
Never report "probably correct." Report only things you can confirm are wrong by checking the
actual lines of code.
Write fixes in a concrete, directly-applicable form (the correct node ID / the edge that should
be added / the correct function name and line number).

Verification theme: {{specify one end-to-end chain. Example:
  Whether "order → payment → async post-processing" is actually connected at the node-ID level.
  Enumerate any broken links (one side created an ID that the other side recreated under a
  different name / a return leg is missing).}}
```

Return value schema:

```json
{
  "type": "object",
  "required": ["issues"],
  "properties": {
    "issues": {"type": "array", "items": {
      "type": "object",
      "required": ["severity", "where", "problem", "fix"],
      "properties": {
        "severity": {"enum": ["critical", "high", "medium", "low"]},
        "where":    {"type": "string"},
        "problem":  {"type": "string"},
        "fix":      {"type": "string"},
        "evidence": {"type": "string"}
      }}},
    "missingFlows":    {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}},
    "danglingNodeIds": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}}
  }
}
```

Cut verification themes along "end-to-end chains," not "layers."
Cutting by layer makes each reviewer look only at their own layer and miss boundary
breaks — the defect that shows up most often.

Example themes (the standard trio is creation flow / cancellation flow / cross-cutting):
1. Creation flow: input → payment/confirmation → save → async post-processing, followed all
   the way through until it actually completes.
2. Cancellation flow: cancel → compensating action (refund/rollback) → retry-on-failure,
   followed all the way down to where it goes dead.
3. Settings changes / scheduled jobs, plus ID hygiene across all files.
